      {
        "id": 138179,
        "name": "dclietz",
        "username": "dclietz",
        "avatar_template": "/letter_avatar_proxy/v4/letter/d/3ec8ea/{size}.png",
        "created_at": "2015-03-28T23:09:14.000Z",
        "cooked": "<p>I think most of the decisions that SotM villains present are related more to (tactical) threat assessment than particular strategies. You often have to decide how to pursue the strategy of your hero deck in light of the threats presented (for example, is the current threat level bad enough that I should play a one-shot to help bring it down or should I play an ongoing to prepare for future threats).</p><p>That said, many villans have at least a few cards or a flip mechanic that impact your strategy. For Omnitron, knowing that he has Terraforming, Bomb, Flechettes, and Singularity&nbsp;<span>makes a big difference in how we play. For Dawn the flip mechanic has a big impact. For Vengeance 5, you often want to choose a villain deck to focus down so you can start turning the tide. I think at least a paragraph or two could be written up about most villains (and some environments) with regards to strategy. </span></p><p><span>Still, most of the strategy part of the game will be implementing your hero's strategy.&nbsp;</span></p>",
        "post_number": 4,
        "post_type": 1,
        "updated_at": "2021-02-07T04:14:40.435Z",
